// A FIELD DEFAULT THAT CALLS A FUNCTION — REFUSED, and the refusal is
// the whole test.
//
// `= default` is emitted at EVERY construction that omitted the field,
// written in the DECLARING module's context. So the expression may name
// no function and read no frame, and that restriction is deliberate:
// emitting `five()` here would mean emitting a call the declaring module
// can resolve into a site that may not be able to.
//
// The restriction was already there. What was NOT there was the
// diagnostic: `gen_c_build.pure_default` answered a default it could not
// write with `Ok(0)` — no initialiser, no complaint — so the field fell
// back to C's `{0}` zeroing and READ BACK 0. That program compiled clean
// and exited 0, which is the failure mode this repo has learned to fear:
// a wrong answer that looks plausible survives every oracle. The SAME
// predicate on a module-level constant reported; only this arm was mute.
//
// tests/corpus/codegen/field_defaults.zen could not see it, because
// every default it declares is a literal or a construction — the shapes
// that PASS the gate. A test whose inputs all pass a gate cannot tell
// the gate from its absence.
//
// tests/corpus/codegen/null_pointer_field_default.zen is the other half:
// `null_ptr<T>()` is the one call a default may make, and it must stay
// legal or `Vec` — and every collection above it — stops compiling.
//
// position: the default expression, not the member and not the struct.

five = () i64 { 5 }

Slot = { n*:: i64 = five() }

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    println("{}", Slot().n);
    Ok(0);
}
